This format stores octree metadata in a separate text header file containing compression type, grid size, node count, and data position. It solves the problem of persistent storage for CPU-based ray casting by separating structural metadata from voxel data, allowing the renderer to map memory efficiently without parsing complex binary headers for every access. It differs from file-system-based indexing methods (like Octree-based Space Subdivision with File System Indexing) by storing the entire octree structure in a single contiguous file rather than distributing nodes across the directory hierarchy. Input: An in-memory octree structure containing node data and leaf voxel Morton codes. Output: An ASCII header file followed by binary data blocks. The format relies on the OctreeInfo metadata structure. OctreeInfo Layout: version: int (Default: 1) basefilename: string (Used to locate the binary data file) gridlength: sizet (Default: 1024) nnodes: sizet (Count of internal octree nodes) ndata: sizet (Count of leaf voxel data entries) 1. Open the header file for writing. 2. Write the magic string #octreeheader 1 followed by a newline. 3. Write gridlength <value> followed by a newline. 4. Write nnodes <value> followed by a newline. 5. Write ndata <value> followed by a newline. 6. Write END followed by a newline. 7. Write a binary block of size VOXELDATASIZE containing the Morton codes for all leaf voxels. 8. Write a binary block containing nnodes entries, each consisting of three sizet values representing node data. 1. Open the header file for reading. 2. Read the first line. Verify it starts with #octreeheader. Extract the version integer. 3. Loop through subsequent lines reading key-value pairs. 4. If the key is gridlength, parse the integer value into info.gridlength. 5. If the key is nnodes, parse the integer value into info.nnodes. 6. If the key is ndata, parse the integer value into info.ndata. 7. If the key is END, stop parsing. 8. If the key is unknown, skip the line. 9. Read a binary block of size VOXELDATASIZE to retrieve the Morton codes. 10. Read nnodes entries, each consisting of three sizet values, to reconstruct the octree node structure. Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. import struct def writeoctreeheader(filepath, gridlength, nnodes, ndata, voxelmortons, nodedata): """Writes the Octree File I/O Format to disk.""" with open(filepath, 'wb') as f: # 1. Write ASCII Header f.write(b'#octreeheader 1
') f.write(f'gridlength {gridlength}
'.encode('ascii')) f.write(f'nnodes {nnodes}
'.encode('ascii')) f.write(f'ndata {ndata}
'.encode('ascii')) f.write(b'END
') # 2. Write Voxel Data # Source specifies a binary block of size VOXELDATASIZE containing morton codes. # We pack them as 64-bit unsigned integers. mortonsize = 8 voxeldatasize = len(voxelmortons) mortonsize for morton in voxelmortons: f.write(struct.pack('Q', morton)) # 3. Write Node Data # Source specifies three sizet values per node. for node in nodedata: for val in node: f.write(struct.pack('Q', val)) def readoctreeheader(filepath): """Reads the Octree File I/O Format from disk.""" info = {'version': 1, 'gridlength': 1024, 'nnodes': 0, 'ndata': 0, 'voxelmortons': [], 'nodedata': []} with open(filepath, 'rb') as f: # 1. Parse Header line = f.readline().decode('ascii').strip() if not line.startswith('#octreeheader'): raise ValueError("Invalid octree header") info['version'] = int(line.split()[1]) while True: line = f.readline().decode('ascii').strip() if line == 'END': break if not line: continue parts = line.split() key = parts[0] if key == 'gridlength': info['gridlength'] = int(parts[1]) elif key == 'nnodes': info['nnodes'] = int(parts[1]) elif key == 'ndata': info['ndata'] = int(parts[1]) else: # Unknown keywords are skipped pass # 2. Read Voxel Data mortonsize = 8 voxeldatasize = info['ndata'] mortonsize voxelbytes = f.read(voxeldatasize) info['voxelmortons'] = list(struct.unpack(f'{info["ndata"]}Q', voxelbytes)) # 3. Read Node Data sizetsize = 8 nodeentries = info['nnodes'] nodebytes = f.read(nodeentries 3 sizetsize) values = list(struct.unpack(f'{nodeentries 3}Q', nodebytes)) info['nodedata'] = [tuple(values[i:i+3]) for i in range(0, len(values), 3)] return info gridlength: Default 1024. version: Default 1. Unknown Keywords: The parser must skip any keyword not recognized as gridlength, nnodes, ndata, or END to maintain forward compatibility. Binary Endianness: The source does not specify the endianness of the binary blocks (VOXELDATASIZE and node data). Implementers must assume a consistent endianness or document the choice, as mismatched endianness will corrupt Morton codes and node pointers. Size Type: sizet size varies by architecture (32-bit vs 64-bit). The file format must be consistent with the architecture that reads it, or the file must specify the pointer size. A data structure and access method for large volumetric datasets that uses an octree for space subdivision and the Unix file system for storage, replacing traditional databases or slice formats to allow instant access to data blocks. At each level of the octree, the position of a voxel is determined using three binary decisions: left/right, front/back, and top/bottom. The octree traversal path is encoded in the file name, which consists of a sequence of numbers (0-7), where each number represents the index of the child node taken at that level of the tree. Empty areas are not stored in the octree structure, saving disk space and RAM, determined in a preprocessing step. Unlike the Octreemizer [38] which only uses octree subdivision without compression, this method incorporates wavelet compression to handle datasets exceeding RAM. Traditional slice formats are too slow because they require opening too many files to display a 3D volume from cross-sections. Serializes and deserializes individual octree nodes to/from a binary file stream. Writing calls fwrite to output three sizet values starting from mData to the file; if compression is not NONE, it calls WriteNodeCompress, and if mData.size() >= 1000, it calls CompressFileIncremental(false). Reading calls fread to input three sizet values into mData. Time Complexity: Parsing the header is O(1) relative to the data size, as it depends only on the number of metadata fields. Reading the binary data is O(N) where N is the number of nodes and voxels. Memory Complexity: The format requires loading the entire binary payload into memory to reconstruct the octree, unless the reader implements streaming or memory mapping. The OctreeInfo structure itself is small. Strengths: Simple ASCII header allows for easy debugging and manual inspection. Binary payload is compact. Limitations: The entire file must be accessible. The lack of specified endianness or exact binary layout for VOXELDATASIZE makes it fragile across platforms. The format is described in the context of a CPU-based voxel raycaster implementation. The file-system-based variant is described in the context of gigascale multiresolution volume rendering on distributed display clusters, aiming to overcome the slowness of traditional slice formats. Forceflow/cpuvoxelraycaster, https://github.com/Forceflow/cpuvoxelraycaster, fetched 2026-10-24 (unofficial) GilesPenfold/MasterThesisSVO, https://github.com/GilesPenfold/MasterThesisSVO, fetched 2026-10-26 (unofficial) Giga-Scale Multiresolution Volume Rendering on Distributed Display Clusters, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-19641-610, fetched 2026-10-26

Exports voxelized geometry data to standard file formats for interchange with other graphics applications. It implements two primary output modes: the binvox format, which uses a compact binary run-length encoding for occupancy data, and the rawvox format, which provides a simple binary dump of grid dimensions and metadata. Unlike its sibling Binary Voxel RLE Encoding which focuses on the internal compression algorithm, this method handles the specific file headers, coordinate transformations, and I/O protocols required by external tools. The method supports two distinct output formats: binvox (binary, sparse) and rawvox (binary, dense metadata). Both require a source voxel grid with defined dimensions, bounding box, and unit size. Inputs: sizex, sizey, sizez: Integer grid dimensions. lb: Lower bound coordinates of the bounding box (Vec3f). unit: Voxel unit sizes (Vec3f). bound: Bounding box object providing a norm() method for normalization. Filled(x, y, z): Boolean function returning true if the voxel at integer coordinates (x, y, z) is occupied. OutFilePath(): Path string for the output file. Outputs: A binary file containing either binvox or rawvox data. Binvox Header: Text-based ASCII lines defining format version, dimensions, translation, and scale. Binvox Data: Binary stream of pairs (value, count). value: 1 byte (unsigned char), 1 if occupied, 0 if empty. count: 1 byte (unsigned char), number of consecutive voxels with the same value. Rawvox Header: Binary stream of floats. First 3 floats: sizex, sizey, sizez. Next 3 floats: lb[0], lb[1], lb[2]. Next 3 floats: unit[0], unit[1], unit[2]. The binvox format stores data in row-major order (X, then Y, then Z) with normalization. 1. Calculate Normalization Parameters: Calculate normtranslate from lb. Calculate normscale from bound.norm(). 2. Write Header: Open file in binary mode. Write line 1: #binvox 1
. Write line 2: dim {bx} {by} {bz}
 where bx=size\x, by=size\y, bz=size\z. Write line 3: translate {-norm	ranslate[0]} {-norm	ranslate[2]} {-norm	ranslate[1]}
. Write line 4: scale {norm\scale}
. Write line 5: data
. 3. Encode Data: Initialize x = lx, y = ly, z = lz (grid bounds). Initialize totalones = 0, byteswritten = 0. Loop while x \le ux: Get value = Filled(x, y, z). Initialize count = 0. Inner Loop: While x \le ux AND count < 255 AND value == Filled(x, y, z): Increment z. If z > uz: Reset z = lz. Increment y. If y > uy: Reset y = ly. Increment x. Increment count. If value is true, totalones += count. Write value (1 byte) and count (1 byte) to file. byteswritten += 2. 4. Close File. The rawvox format is a simpler binary dump of metadata. 1. Open File: Open output file in binary mode. 2. Write Header: Write sizex. Write sizey. Write sizez. Write lb[0], lb[1], lb[2] (cast to double/float). Write unit[0], unit[1], unit[2] (cast to double/float). 3. Close File. A companion text-based format often used with rawvox. 1. Derive Path: Strip.rawvox or.binvox extension from base output path and append.meta. 2. Open File: Open output file in binary mode. 3. Write Header: Write #voxmeta 1
. Write sizex sizey sizez
. 4. Write Voxel Data: Iterate voxelindex from 0 to 	ext{TotalVoxelSize()} - 1. Write voxelindex and flags separated by space, followed by newline. 5. Close File. Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def writebinvox(outpath, sizex, sizey, sizez, lb, bound, filledfunc): """ Writes a voxel grid to the binvox format. lb: tuple (lbx, lby, lbz) bound: object with norm() method returning a scalar for normalization filledfunc: callable(x, y, z) -> bool """ # 1. Calculate normalization parameters per S1 normtranslate = lb normscale = bound.norm() # 2. Write Header with open(outpath, 'wb') as f: f.write(b'#binvox 1
') f.write(f'dim {sizex} {sizey} {sizez}
'.encode('ascii')) # Y and Z components of translation are swapped in the header per S1 f.write(f'translate {-normtranslate[0]} {-normtranslate[2]} {-normtranslate[1]}
'.encode('ascii')) f.write(f'scale {normscale}
'.encode('ascii')) f.write(b'data
') # 3. Encode Data lx, ly, lz = 0, 0, 0 ux, uy, uz = sizex - 1, sizey - 1, sizez - 1 x, y, z = lx, ly, lz while x <= ux: value = 1 if filledfunc(x, y, z) else 0 count = 0 # Inner loop: run-length encode consecutive voxels with same value while x <= ux and count < 255 and (1 if filledfunc(x, y, z) else 0) == value: z += 1 if z > uz: z = lz y += 1 if y > uy: y = ly x += 1 count += 1 # Write value and count as unsigned chars f.write(bytes([value, count])) return def writerawvox(outpath, sizex, sizey, sizez, lb, unit): """ Writes voxel grid metadata to rawvox format. """ with open(outpath, 'wb') as f: # Write dimensions as floats f.write(float(sizex).tobytes(4, 'little') + float(sizey).tobytes(4, 'little') + float(sizez).tobytes(4, 'little')) # Write lower bound f.write(float(lb[0]).tobytes(4, 'little') + float(lb[1]).tobytes(4, 'little') + float(lb[2]).tobytes(4, 'little')) # Write unit size f.write(float(unit[0]).tobytes(4, 'little') + float(unit[1]).tobytes(4, 'little') + float(unit[2]).tobytes(4, 'little')) Exports voxel particles to the MagicaVoxel.vox binary format, handling grid sizing and chunk structure. It differs from generic voxel I/O by enforcing the specific chunk-based binary layout and resolution limits required by the MagicaVoxel application. Choose it when targeting MagicaVoxel workflows. A loader for MagicaVoxel.vox files (versions 150 and 200) that parses models, palette, MATL materials, and the nTRN/nGRP/nSHP scene graph, converting visible faces into quads within a BVH. It structures data into chunks containing tags, sizes, and child chunks. Unlike raw voxel grids, this method extracts surface geometry (quads) for ray tracing while supporting complex scene hierarchies and coordinate transformations. Choose it when importing MagicaVoxel assets for rendering. PLY File Parsing extracts vertex attributes and topology from Polygon File Format (PLY) files, serving as the bridge between raw file storage and in-memory voxel or mesh data structures. It solves the problem of heterogeneous PLY headers (ASCII vs. binary, varying property types) by normalizing the input into a consistent vertex buffer and face index array. Unlike Voxel File I/O (binvox/rawvox) which handles volumetric grids, this method processes surface representations; it differs from MagicaVoxel File Parsing by supporting arbitrary vertex properties and standard triangle/quad topology rather than a proprietary voxel color format. Choose it when converting mesh assets to voxelization pipelines. Voxel File I/O is the method for serializing and deserializing voxel grid data to and from disk storage formats. It solves the problem of persisting volumetric representations generated by voxelization or optimization pipelines for later use in rendering engines or analysis tools. Unlike Voxel File I/O (binvox/rawvox), which focuses on specific legacy binary and raw byte formats, this method encompasses a broader range of structured formats including ASCII-based scientific formats (VTK, PLY, CSV), specialized scientific image formats (PFM, NRRD-like), and custom binary schemas. Choose it when requiring cross-platform scientific data exchange. Time Complexity: O(N) where N is the number of voxels in the bounding box, as every voxel must be checked for occupancy. The binvox encoding step is also linear in the number of runs. Memory Complexity: O(1) auxiliary memory for the writer, as it streams data. The input grid must be stored in memory. Performance: Binvox is significantly smaller in file size for sparse grids due to RLE. Rawvox is fast to write but produces larger files if metadata is dense. Strengths: Standardized formats for interchange. Binvox is widely supported by 3D modeling tools. Limitations: Binvox is binary and opaque without a parser. Rawvox lacks color/texture data in the basic header. Coordinate normalization in binvox can introduce precision issues if not handled carefully. The binvox format was developed as a compact binary format for storing 3D voxel data, particularly for use in computer graphics and 3D modeling. It is widely used in datasets like ShapeNet and by tools like MagicaVoxel. The rawvox format is a simpler, less standardized binary format often used in research voxelizers for quick debugging or intermediate storage. The implementation described here originates from the topskychen/voxelizer project, which provides a C++ voxelization pipeline. topskychen/voxelizer, https://github.com/topskychen/voxelizer, fetched 2026-10-27 (unofficial)

This method defines a minimal, custom binary volume file format designed for efficient storage and transfer of voxel grids. It solves the problem of serializing 3D volumetric data by using a simple two-line header followed by a raw binary payload. Unlike the full Network Common Data Form (NRRD) specification, which supports complex metadata, encodings, and transformations, this variant is restricted to fixed dimensions and a single data type (uint32t), prioritizing implementation simplicity and parsing speed over flexibility. It serves as a lightweight alternative to more verbose formats like VTK Legacy ASCII File Writing or the more complex NRRD File Parsing and Volume Initialization. Inputs and Outputs: Input: A contiguous array of uint32t values representing the voxel grid, along with its dimensions (Dx, Dy, Dz). Output: A binary file containing the header and raw voxel data. Input (Import): A file in the specified format. Output: A resized array of uint32t and the parsed dimensions. Data Structures: The file layout is strictly sequential: 1. Line 1: Three space-separated integers representing Dx (width), Dy (height), and Dz (depth). 2. Line 2: The literal string uint32 followed by a newline. 3. Payload: Raw binary bytes of the voxel data, stored in row-major order (X fastest, then Y, then Z). The total size is Dx 	imes Dy 	imes Dz 	imes 4 bytes. Numbered Steps: 1. Export: 1. Open the output file for writing in binary mode. 2. Write the dimensions as a string: f"{Dx} {Dy} {Dz}
". 3. Write the data type identifier: "uint32
". 4. Write the raw voxel data. The data array mdata is treated as a byte stream. 	ext{bytes	o\write} = Dx 	imes Dy 	imes Dz 	imes 	ext{sizeof(uint32	)} Write mdata directly to the file buffer. 2. Import: 1. Open the input file for reading in binary mode. 2. Read the first line as text. Parse the three integers to obtain Dx, Dy, Dz. 3. Read the second line as text. Verify it equals "uint32". If not, raise an error or handle as unsupported. 4. Calculate the total voxel count: N = Dx 	imes Dy 	imes Dz 5. Allocate or resize the output array mdata to hold N elements of type uint32t. 6. Read N 	imes 	ext{sizeof(uint32	)} bytes from the file into mdata. 7. Verify that the read operation consumed the expected number of bytes. If the file ends prematurely, handle the error. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. import struct def exportnrrdlike(filename: str, dims: tuple, data: list): """ Exports a volume to the NRRD-like format.:param filename: Output file path.:param dims: Tuple (Dx, Dy, Dz) of integers.:param data: List of uint32t values. """ Dx, Dy, Dz = dims voxelcount = Dx Dy Dz if len(data) != voxelcount: raise ValueError("Data length does not match dimensions") with open(filename, 'wb') as f: # Write header line 1: dimensions f.write(f"{Dx} {Dy} {Dz}
".encode('ascii')) # Write header line 2: data type f.write(b"uint32
") # Write raw binary payload # Pack as little-endian unsigned 32-bit integers # Note: The source uses reinterpretcast, implying native byte order. # For portability, explicit packing is safer, but we follow the source's # assumption of direct memory copy if endianness matches. # Here we use struct to ensure consistent binary output. packeddata = struct.pack(f'{voxelcount}I', data) f.write(packeddata) def importnrrdlike(filename: str) -> tuple: """ Imports a volume from the NRRD-like format.:param filename: Input file path.:return: Tuple (dims, data) where dims is (Dx, Dy, Dz) and data is list of uint32t. """ with open(filename, 'rb') as f: # Read header line 1 line1 = f.readline().decode('ascii').strip() parts = line1.split() if len(parts) != 3: raise ValueError("Invalid header line 1") Dx, Dy, Dz = int(parts[0]), int(parts[1]), int(parts[2]) # Read header line 2 line2 = f.readline().decode('ascii').strip() if line2 != "uint32": raise ValueError(f"Unsupported data type: {line2}") voxelcount = Dx Dy Dz bytestoread = voxelcount 4 # sizeof(uint32t) = 4 # Read raw binary payload rawbytes = f.read(bytestoread) if len(rawbytes) != bytestoread: raise ValueError("File truncated: insufficient data") # Unpack as little-endian unsigned 32-bit integers data = struct.unpack(f'{voxelcount}I', rawbytes) return (Dx, Dy, Dz), list(data) Parameters: Data Type: Fixed to uint32t. No other types are supported by this specific variant. Endianness: The source code uses reinterpretcast, which implies native machine endianness. Implementers should be aware that files generated on little-endian machines may not read correctly on big-endian machines without conversion. For cross-platform compatibility, explicit endianness handling (e.g., swapping bytes if necessary) should be added. Edge Cases and Pitfalls: Truncated Files: The import step must verify that the number of bytes read matches the expected size. Failure to do so may result in reading garbage data or crashing. Dimension Mismatch: The number of elements in the data array must exactly equal Dx 	imes Dy 	imes Dz. Header Parsing: Whitespace handling in the first header line should be robust (e.g., splitting by any whitespace). Time Complexity: Export: O(N), where N is the number of voxels. Writing the header is O(1). Import: O(N), dominated by reading the binary payload. Memory Complexity: O(N) for storing the voxel data. The header requires negligible memory. Performance: This format is extremely fast to parse and write because it avoids complex header parsing, metadata extraction, and encoding/decoding steps. It is suitable for intermediate data exchange in pipelines where speed is critical and metadata is handled separately. Strengths: Simplicity, small file size (no overhead for metadata or compression), fast I/O. Limitations: No support for metadata (spacing, orientation, data type flexibility), no compression, no support for multiple data types or arrays. Not suitable for long-term archival or interoperability with general-purpose scientific tools that expect standard NRRD. When to use: Internal pipeline data transfer, debugging, or scenarios where the volume structure is known and fixed. When not to use: When metadata (physical spacing, coordinate systems) is required, or when interoperability with standard medical/scientific imaging software is needed. This format is an unofficial, simplified variant of the Network Common Data Form (NRRD) specification. It originates from the max-pio/compressed-segmentation-volumes repository, where it is used as a lightweight container for voxel data in the context of compressed segmentation volumes. It is not a standard format defined by any governing body but rather a pragmatic convention adopted by specific implementations to avoid the complexity of full NRRD parsers. max-pio/compressed-segmentation-volumes, https://github.com/max-pio/compressed-segmentation-volumes, fetched 2026-10-26 (unofficial)

Exports voxel data to a legacy VTK ASCII UNSTRUCTUREDGRID file format, representing each voxel as a hexahedral cell with associated scalar fields. It differs from binary voxel formats (e.g., Binvox File Parsing) by using human-readable text and explicit vertex definitions for every cell, prioritizing interoperability with legacy VTK-based viewers over storage efficiency. Inputs: A voxel dataset containing bounding box coordinates (boxmin, boxmax), cell count, and multiple scalar fields (e.g., voidratio, particlecount). Outputs: A.vtk file in ASCII format. Data Structures: The output is a VTK UNSTRUCTUREDGRID. POINTS: A list of 3D coordinates. Total count = cellcount 8. CELLS: A flat list defining connectivity. Each cell entry consists of 9 integers: 8 (number of vertices) followed by 8 vertex indices. CELLTYPES: A list of integers. Each entry is 11 (VTKHEXAHEDRON). CELLDATA: Scalar arrays associated with each cell. Steps: 1. Header: Write the VTK magic number and version: # vtk DataFile Version 2.0 <title> ASCII DATASET UNSTRUCTUREDGRID 2. POINTS: Write the point count and type: POINTS {pointcount} float Compute the 8 vertices for each cell using the global bounding box (boxmin, boxmax) and a local index i \in [0, 7]. The local index maps to the 8 corners of the hexahedron. For a cell with bounds [x{min}, x{max}] 	imes [y{min}, y{max}] 	imes [z{min}, z{max}], the vertices are: i=0: (x{min}, y{min}, z{min}) i=1: (x{max}, y{min}, z{min}) i=2: (x{min}, y{max}, z{min}) i=3: (x{max}, y{max}, z{min}) i=4: (x{min}, y{min}, z{max}) i=5: (x{max}, y{min}, z{max}) i=6: (x{min}, y{max}, z{max}) i=7: (x{max}, y{max}, z{max}) Write each coordinate formatted as '{:.17g}' to preserve double precision. 3. CELLS: Write the connectivity: CELLS {cellcount} {cellcount 9} For each cell index c from 0 to cellcount - 1: Calculate start = c 8. Write 8 followed by the 8 vertex indices: start, start+1,..., start+7. 4. CELLTYPES: Write the cell type array: CELLTYPES {cellcount} Write the integer 11 for each cell. 5. CELLDATA: Write the scalar fields: CELLDATA {cellcount} For each scalar field (e.g., voidratio, particlecount): Write header: SCALARS {name} {type} 1 Write: LOOKUPTABLE default Write the values, one per line, in the same order as the cells. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def writevtklegacyascii(outputpath, boxmin, boxmax, cellcount, scalarfields): """ boxmin: tuple (x, y, z) boxmax: tuple (x, y, z) cellcount: int scalarfields: dict mapping name -> list of values (length cellcount) """ pointcount = cellcount 8 # Helper to get 8 vertices for a cell given its local bounds # Note: In the source, boxmin/boxmax are global, but typically # voxels have individual bounds. The source implies a single box # or pre-computed points. We assume the source logic computes # vertices based on local cell bounds derived from global box and dims. # However, the source snippet shows: # def pointtuple(boundsmin, boundsmax, localindex): # We must assume boundsmin/max are passed or computed per cell. # For a general implementation, we need cell bounds. # The source uses boxmin/boxmax directly in the snippet context, # but logically, each cell has its own min/max. # We will write the structure assuming we have a function getcellbounds(c). def getcellbounds(c): # Placeholder for logic not fully detailed in source snippet # but implied by "Compute 8 vertices per cell using boxmin/boxmax and local index" # If the entire grid is one box, this is trivial. # If it's a grid, we need dims. # We will assume the caller provides cellbounds or we compute them. # For the sake of the article, we assume cellbounds is available. pass with open(outputpath, 'w') as f: # 1. Header f.write("# vtk DataFile Version 2.0
") f.write("Voxel Data
") # Title f.write("ASCII
") f.write("DATASET UNSTRUCTUREDGRID
") # 2. POINTS f.write(f"POINTS {pointcount} float
") # We need to generate all points. # The source computes points per cell. # To avoid duplication, we can store unique points or just write them. # VTK UNSTRUCTUREDGRID allows duplicate points if indices are used. # The source writes 8 points per cell. for c in range(cellcount): # Assume cellmin and cellmax are known for cell c # If not provided in source, this is a gap # We assume a function getcellbounds(c) returns (min, max) cellmin, cellmax = getcellbounds(c) for localidx in range(8): # Map localidx to corner # 0: min, min, min # 1: max, min, min # 2: min, max, min # 3: max, max, min # 4: min, min, max # 5: max, min, max # 6: min, max, max # 7: max, max, max x = cellmin[0] if (localidx & 1) == 0 else cellmax[0] y = cellmin[1] if (localidx & 2) == 0 else cellmax[1] z = cellmin[2] if (localidx & 4) == 0 else cellmax[2] f.write(f"{x:.17g} {y:.17g} {z:.17g}
") # 3. CELLS f.write(f"CELLS {cellcount} {cellcount 9}
") for c in range(cellcount): start = c 8 f.write(f"8 {start} {start+1} {start+2} {start+3} {start+4} {start+5} {start+6} {start+7}
") # 4. CELLTYPES f.write(f"CELLTYPES {cellcount}
") for c in range(cellcount): f.write("11
") # 5. CELLDATA f.write(f"CELLDATA {cellcount}
") for name, values in scalarfields.items(): # Determine type if isinstance(values[0], float): vtype = "double" else: vtype = "int" f.write(f"SCALARS {name} {vtype} 1
") f.write("LOOKUPTABLE default
") for val in values: f.write(f"{val}
") Parameters: pointcount: Total vertices = cellcount 8. cellcount: Number of voxels. Edge Cases: Precision: Coordinates must be formatted with high precision ({:.17g}) to avoid rounding errors in VTK readers. Empty Grids: If cellcount is 0, the file should still contain valid headers but no data sections. A variant used in the Grasshopper environment that serializes VoxelGrid objects into a custom chunk-based format rather than standard VTK ASCII. It removes the 'PersistentData' chunk, writes a 'GridCount' Int32, and iterates through persistent data branches. For each grid, it creates a 'Grid' chunk, converts the voxel grid to bytes, compresses them, converts to a hex string, and writes it to the 'Data' field along with the GHPath. Reading involves reversing this process: reading the count, finding the 'Grid' chunk by index, reading the hex string, decompressing, and reconstructing the VoxelGrid. Time Complexity: O(N) where N is the number of cells, as each cell and its vertices are written sequentially. Memory Complexity: O(N) to store the output buffer or file. Performance: Very slow for large datasets due to ASCII overhead and lack of compression. Suitable for small voxel grids or debugging. Strengths: Human-readable, easy to parse with standard text tools, compatible with legacy VTK pipelines. Limitations: Large file sizes, slow I/O, no support for complex geometries beyond hexahedra. When to use: When interoperability with legacy VTK tools is required and dataset size is small. When not to use: For large-scale volume rendering or real-time applications where I/O speed is critical. The VTK Legacy ASCII format is part of the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) file formats, specifically the UNSTRUCTUREDGRID representation. It is used in point-cloud voxelization workflows to export voxel data for visualization. The format relies on the VTK Hexahedron cell type (ID 11) to represent voxels. SammySuliman/point-cloud-voxelization, https://github.com/SammySuliman/point-cloud-