Pixel Intensity Evaluation with Scene Aggregates is a method for rendering volumetric data that replaces order-dependent ray marching with a precomputed visibility aggregation model. It solves the problem of correctly blending scene aggregate contributions with external background objects while handling inter-voxel correlation, which traditional volumetric models fail to distinguish. Unlike sibling schemes such as Intensity or Accumulate that process samples sequentially, this method evaluates pixel intensity by integrating radiance over the pixel footprint using Aggregated Visibility Functions (AVF), specifically the Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV). The method evaluates pixel intensity by integrating incoming radiance over the pixel footprint, utilizing precomputed visibility terms to handle occlusion and transparency without per-ray traversal. Inputs: Scene geometry represented as a set of primitives (e.g., truncated ellipsoids) derived from voxels. Pixel footprint P in world space. Pixel direction  oldsymbol{
u}p. Background radiance Lb (assumed constant due to far-field assumption). Precomputed Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV) data. Outputs: Pixel intensity I. The core data structure is the Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV) term, stored as wavelet coefficients. ABV Representation: The ABV term \hat{V}b is defined on the 2D boundary enclosing the 3D scene aggregate. Storage: The visibility function is projected to the Haar wavelet basis to allow for compression and efficient reconstruction. Resolution: The angular resolution for the precomputed ABV is typically 64^2 to accurately reconstruct the high-frequency silhouette signal. 1. Scene Discretization: Convert the volumetric scene into a set of primitives (e.g., truncated ellipsoids) associated with voxels. 2. Boundary Face Identification: Determine the list of boundary faces of the voxels using a flood fill iteration on the discretized scene. 3. ABV Precomputation: For each boundary face, perform the following sub-steps: a. Consider all directions in the inward-facing hemisphere of the face. b. Parameterize the hemispherical domain using concentric mapping to obtain a 2D domain. c. Cast visibility rays with origins uniformly sampled on the face for each direction to estimate average visibility. d. Project the resulting 2D visibility map to the Haar wavelet basis. 4. Pixel Intensity Evaluation: For a given pixel, calculate the intensity I by integrating the incoming radiance Li over the pixel footprint P: I =  rac{1}{|P|} \intP Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) d oldsymbol{x}p where |P| is the area of P and  oldsymbol{
u}p is the pixel direction. 5. Radiance Determination: Determine Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) for each point  oldsymbol{x}p in P based on whether it is covered by scene primitives: Let P^+ be the subset of P covered by the primitive(s) (i.e., rays from P^+ intersect the scene aggregate). Let P^- = P \setminus P^+ be the subset not covered. If  oldsymbol{x}p \in P^-, Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) = Lb(- oldsymbol{
u}p) (background radiance). If  oldsymbol{x}p \in P^+, Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) is evaluated based on the primitive's outgoing radiance, modulated by the visibility. The Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV) is defined as: \hat{V}b( oldsymbol{
u}; P^+) =  rac{1}{|P|} \int{P^+} V( oldsymbol{x},  oldsymbol{
u}) d oldsymbol{x} where: P is the pixel footprint in world space. P^+ is the subset of P such that rays from P^+ intersect scene aggregate primitives. |P| is the area of P. V( oldsymbol{x},  oldsymbol{
u}) is the visibility at point  oldsymbol{x} in direction  oldsymbol{
u}. The pixel intensity is calculated as: I =  rac{1}{|P|} \intP Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) d oldsymbol{x}p Angular Resolution: 64^2 samples per hemisphere for ABV precomputation. Mapping: Concentric mapping is used to parameterize the hemispherical domain. Basis: Haar wavelet basis is used for projecting the visibility map. Inter-voxel Correlation: Traditional volumetric models cannot distinguish between different types of inter-voxel correlation (e.g., clustering vs. uniform distribution) if local properties are identical. This method handles this by using aggregated visibility functions. High-Frequency Silhouettes: The ABV term contains high-frequency signals responsible for reconstructing the silhouette of the scene. Insufficient angular resolution (e.g., lower than 64^2) may lead to inaccurate coverage reconstruction. Far-Field Assumption: The method assumes a far-field background, meaning background radiance Lb does not depend on position. Time Complexity: Precomputation is expensive, requiring flood fill, ray casting for visibility, and wavelet projection for all boundary faces. However, runtime evaluation is efficient as it avoids per-ray marching, relying instead on integration over the pixel footprint using precomputed data. Memory Complexity: Requires storage for the wavelet coefficients of the ABV (and potentially AIV) terms for all boundary faces. The resolution of 64^2 per face contributes to memory usage. Strengths: Correctly blends scene aggregates with background; handles inter-voxel correlation; avoids order-dependent ray marching. Limitations: Requires precomputation; relies on far-field assumption for background; accuracy depends on angular resolution. When to use: Suitable for level-of-detail rendering where efficient evaluation of complex volumetric scenes with accurate background blending is required. When not to use: When dynamic scenes prevent precomputation or when near-field background effects are critical. The method was introduced in the paper "Efficient Scene Appearance Aggregation for Level-of-Detail Rendering". It addre