Clough–Tocher Bézier Patch Interpolation replaces linear barycentric interpolation with a cubic Bézier patch construction to interpolate feature values and gradients over a triangle, guaranteeing C1 continuity everywhere and C∞ within each triangle. It solves the problem of visible seam artifacts caused by gradient discontinuities across triangle edges in fully opaque meshes, which arise because standard barycentric interpolation produces a C0 feature field. Unlike its parent method, Barycentric Interpolation on Triangles, which only uses vertex values, this method requires per-vertex gradients to define the patch geometry. Inputs: Triangle vertices \mathbf{v}0, \mathbf{v}1, \mathbf{v}2. Feature values at vertices f0, f1, f2. Feature gradients at vertices 
abla f0, 
abla f1, 
abla f2. Evaluation point \mathbf{p} in 3D space (used to compute barycentric coordinates). Outputs: Interpolated feature value f(\mathbf{p}). (Optional) Interpolated gradient 
abla f(\mathbf{p}) if the derivative of the Bézier patch is required. Data Structures: Bézier Control Points: An array of 10 scalar (or vector) values c{ijk} where i+j+k=3. The layout is: Vertices: c{300}, c{030}, c{003} Edges: c{210}, c{120}, c{021}, c{012}, c{102}, c{201} Center: c{111} Steps: 1. Compute Edge Vectors: Calculate the edge vectors for the triangle: \mathbf{e}{01} = \mathbf{v}1 - \mathbf{v}0 \mathbf{e}{12} = \mathbf{v}2 - \mathbf{v}1 \mathbf{e}{20} = \mathbf{v}0 - \mathbf{v}2 2. Compute Bézier Control Points: Define the 10 control points using the vertex values, gradients, and edge vectors: c{300} = f0 c{030} = f1 c{003} = f2 c{210} = f0 +  rac{1}{3} 
abla f0 \cdot \mathbf{e}{01} c{120} = f1 -  rac{1}{3} 
abla f1 \cdot \mathbf{e}{01} c{021} = f1 +  rac{1}{3} 
abla f1 \cdot \mathbf{e}{12} c{012} = f2 -  rac{1}{3} 
abla f2 \cdot \mathbf{e}{12} c{102} = f2 +  rac{1}{3} 
abla f2 \cdot \mathbf{e}{20} c{201} = f0 -  rac{1}{3} 
abla f0 \cdot \mathbf{e}{20} c{111} =  rac{1}{6} (c{210} + c{120} + c{021} + c{012} + c{102} + c{201}) -  rac{1}{6} (c{300} + c{030} + c{003}) 3. Compute Barycentric Coordinates: Calculate the barycentric coordinates (\alpha0, \alpha1, \alpha2) of the evaluation point \mathbf{p} relative to the triangle vertices. This is typically done by projecting \mathbf{p} onto the triangle plane or using the area method. Let \alphai be the weight for vertex i. 4. Evaluate Bézier Patch: Compute the interpolated feature value f(\mathbf{p}) using the Bernstein basis polynomials of degree 3: f(\mathbf{p}) = \sum{i+j+k=3}  inom{3}{i,j,k} \alpha0^i \alpha1^j \alpha2^k c{ijk} where  inom{3}{i,j,k} =  rac{3!}{i!j!k!} is the multinomial coefficient. Gradient Estimation (Preprocessing): The method requires per-vertex gradients 
abla fv. These are estimated via a per-vertex least-squares fit over the one-ring neighborhood. For each vertex v, gather all neighbor vertices \{vj\}{j \in N(v)} and solve the 2x2 linear system: 
abla fv = \mathbf{A}v^{-1} \mathbf{b}v where: \mathbf{A}v = \sumj \Delta \mathbf{p}j \Delta \mathbf{p}j^	op \mathbf{b}v = \sumj \Delta fj \Delta \mathbf{p}j Here, \Delta \mathbf{p}j = \mathbf{v}j - \mathbf{v}v and \Delta fj = fj - fv. The sum is over all neighbors j of vertex v. The 2x2 system is solved analytically. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. import math def computebarycentriccoords(v0, v1, v2, p): """ Computes barycentric coordinates of point p relative to triangle v0, v1, v2. Assumes p is coplanar with the triangle or projected onto it. Returns (alpha0, alpha1, alpha2). """ # Vector from v0 to vertices v0v1 = v1 - v0 v0v2 = v2 - v0 v0p = p - v0 # Dot products dot00 = dot(v0v2, v0v2) dot01 = dot(v0v2, v0v1) dot02 = dot(v0v2, v0p) dot11 = dot(v0v1, v0v1) dot12 = dot(v0v1, v0p) # Inverse determinant invdet = 1.0 / (dot00 dot11 - dot01 dot01) # Barycentric coordinates alpha2 = (dot11 dot02 - dot01 dot12) invdet alpha1 = (dot00 dot12 - dot01 dot02) invdet alpha0 = 1.0 - alpha1 - alpha2 return alpha0, alpha1, alpha2 def cloughtocherinterpolate(v0, v1, v2, f0, f1, f2, grad0, grad1, grad2, p): """ Interpolates feature value at point p using Clough-Tocher Bézier patch. Args: v0, v1, v2: Vertex positions (3D vectors) f0, f1, f2: Feature values at vertices (scalars or vectors) grad0, grad1, grad2: Feature gradients at vertices (2D or 3D vectors depending on context) p: Evaluation point (3D vector) Returns: Interpolated feature value """ # 1. Compute Edge Vectors e01 = v1 - v0 e12 = v2 - v1 e20 = v0 - v2 # 2. Compute Control Points # Vertex values c300 = f0 c030 = f1 c003 = f2 # Edge-related points # Note: dot product of gradient and edge vector c210 = f0 + (1.0/3.0) dot(grad0, e01) c120 = f1 - (1.0/3.0) dot(grad1, e01) c021 = f1 + (1.0/3.0) dot(grad1, e12) c012 = f2 - (1.0/3.0) dot(grad2, e12) c102 = f2 + (1.0/3.0) dot(grad2, e20) c201 = f0 - (1.0/3.0) 