" return code Tagging Complexity: Identifying and tagging suitable semantic variables in the input shader can be time-consuming. This process is performed only once by a visualization expert before the domain expert uses the abstract control. Precision: The mapping of composite values to visible color ranges uses a simple multiplication factor. More advanced mappings could be used, but the simple method was found sufficient for the purpose. Dimensionality Mismatch: Membership function textures must match the dimensionality of the data properties (1D texture for 1D attribute, etc.). Mismatches will lead to incorrect sampling. A method for determining the mapping from semantic data properties to visual attributes using rules that are graphically specified and evaluated using fuzzy-logic arithmetics. It adds functionality to evaluate fuzzy visualization rules to the augmented shader, interpreting attributes of interest as fuzzy sets where membership functions describe contributions to the mapping. Unlike formal and textual rule formulations which are considered rigid and non-exploratory, this method combines rule-based rendering with graphical user interfaces for defining multi-dimensional mappings. Visual attributes (output entities) such as color, opacity, or sparseness parameters are mapped based on these rules. Time Complexity: The augmentation process (parsing, code generation, insertion) occurs in main memory before shader loading. The runtime cost is the evaluation of fuzzy rules and membership function lookups during raycasting. The overhead is minimal compared to hard-coded solutions because code fragments are generated on the fly rather than combined from pre-coded libraries. Memory Complexity: Requires storage for the augmented shader source, the semantic-property textures (rendered during Step 1), and the membership function textures. Strengths: Allows quick exploration of different semantic mappings for different visualization techniques without individual