abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 L2 = \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi)^T + (I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt}) 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) \quad (6) On a converged model, the residual term (I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt}) approaches zero, causing the second-order term to vanish. The Hessian approximates the Fisher Information Matrix: 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 L2 \approx \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi)^T \quad (7) The sensitivity score Ui for Gaussian i is defined as the log-determinant of the block-diagonal approximation restricted to the mean \mathbf{x}i and scale \mathbf{s}i parameters: Ui = \log |
abla{\mathbf{x}i, \mathbf{s}i} I{\mathcal{G}} 
