Simple-NeRF is a radiance field regularization method for sparse input views that trains two augmented NeRF models (Smoothing and Lambertian) in tandem with a main NeRF to reduce overfitting and distortions, using depth supervision and consistency losses. It differs from its siblings by explicitly reducing model capacity (via positional encoding and view-dependence removal) to learn "simpler" solutions that serve as reliable depth priors, whereas HashNeRF Parameters focuses on hash table regularization and Gradient Scaling for Near-Camera Training focuses on loss weighting. Inputs and Outputs: Inputs: Sparse set of input images and their camera poses; sparse depth estimates from Structure from Motion (SfM). Outputs: A trained main NeRF model (N1, N2) for inference; augmented models (N{1s}, N{2s}, N{1l}, N{2l}) are discarded after training. Data Structures: The method uses four neural networks: 1. Main Coarse MLP (N1): Input: Positional encoding \gamma(\mathbf{p}i, 0, lp). Output: Density \sigmai and latent feature \mathbf{h}i. 2. Main Fine MLP (N2): Input: Latent feature \mathbf{h}i and view direction \gamma(\mathbf{v}i, 0, lv). Output: Color \mathbf{c}i. 3. Smoothing Augmentation Coarse MLP (N{1s}): Input: Positional encoding \gamma(\mathbf{p}i, l{ps}, lp) (reduced frequency). Output: Latent feature \mathbf{h}{is}. 4. Smoothing Augmentation Fine MLP (N{2s}): Input: Latent feature \mathbf{h}{is} and view direction \gamma(\mathbf{v}i, 0, lv). Output: Color \mathbf{c}{is}. 5. Lambertian Augmentation Coarse MLP (N{1l}): Input: Positional encoding \gamma(\mathbf{p}i, 0, lp). Output: Latent feature \mathbf{h}{il}. 6. Lambertian Augmentation Fine MLP (N{2l}): Input: Latent feature \mathbf{h}{il} and no view direction (or zero vector). Output: Color \mathbf{c}{il}. Note: The positional encoding function is defined as \gamma(\mathbf{x}, d1, d2) = [\sin(2^{d1}\mathbf{x}), \cos(2^{d1}\mathbf{x}), \dots, \sin(2^{d2-1}\mathbf{x}), \cos(2^{d2-1}\mathbf{x})]. If d1=0, \mathbf{x} is concatenated directly. Parameters: lp: Highest positional encoding frequency for position. lv: Highest positional encoding frequency for view direction. l{ps}: Reduced highest positional encoding frequency for the smoothing augmentation coarse MLP. Sampling: Coarse stratified sampling (64 queries) and fine dense sampling (192 queries) per pixel. Numbered Steps: 1. Initialization: Initialize main NeRF (N1, N2) and two augmented models: Smoothing (N{1s}, N{2s}) and Lambertian (N{1l}, N{2l}). 2. Forward Pass (Main): For each pixel, sample points along the ray. Compute density \sigma and color \mathbf{c} using N1 and N2. Render color \hat{C} and mean depth zm. 3. Forward Pass (Augmented): Smoothing: Pass remaining high-frequency positional encoding components \gamma(\mathbf{p}i, l{ps}, lp) to N{1s} to get \mathbf{h}{is}. Pass \mathbf{h}{is} and \gamma(\mathbf{v}i, 0, lv) to N{2s} to get \mathbf{c}{is}. Render coarse depth zs. Lambertian: Pass \gamma(\mathbf{p}i, 0, lp) to N{1l} to get \mathbf{h}{il}. Pass \mathbf{h}{il} and zero view direction to N{2l} to get \mathbf{c}{il}. Render coarse depth zl. 4. Reliability Masking: Compute reliability masks ma (for augmented) and mm (for main) by reprojecting to the closest training view and checking intensity consistency. If intensities do not match (e.g., specular regions), the mask is zero. 5. Depth Supervision Loss (L{aug}): Impose depth supervision using stop-gradient (\hat{
