ments for intersection. Time and Memory Complexity: The optimized algorithm reduces the average number of Plücker tests to 2.67 per tetrahedron. Memory overhead is 16 bytes per tetrahedron for connectivity. Pre-computing Plücker coordinates doubles memory requirements, so on-the-fly conversion is preferred. Measured Performance: Baseline performance: 15.5 million tetrahedra per second on a Dual-core Opteron with 2GHz (both CPUs used). Performance gain: 55% average increase in raw performance compared to the naive approach of testing all three exit faces independently. Tests on an Intel Celeron PC at 900MHz showed the optimized Plücker algorithm outperforms Haines' algorithm and Moller/Trumbore-based algorithms in all tested cases. Haines' algorithm performs comparatively better when few ray-tetrahedron pairs intersect because it can sometimes decide there is no intersection without examining all four faces. Strengths and Limitations: Strengths: Directly provides scaled barycentric coordinates, avoiding division until normalization. Robust and efficient, requiring few floating point operations and no division. Limitations: Cannot apply parametric distance restrictions (e.g., max ray length) early; these must be applied as post-processing. When to Use: Use for ray tracing of tetrahedral meshes where performance and barycentric coordinate computation are critical. Avoid if early rejection of non-intersecting rays is the primary concern and few intersections are expected (Haines' algorithm may be better). Origin: The method is based on the ray-tetrahedron intersection algorithm introduced by Platis et al. [PT03]. Plücker coordinates were used to specify directed lines in three-dimensional space [Eri97]. The approach extends and accelerates an approach suggested by Garrity [Gar90], who used a grid as an acceleration structure and connectivity information to traverse cells incrementally by calculating plane intersections. History and Development: The Plücker-based ray-tetrahedron intersection algorithm was presented by Nikos Platis and Theoharis Theoharis. It was shown to outperform Haines' algorithm and Moller/Trumbore-based algorithms. The method is used in ray tracing of triangular meshes (cited in [AC97] and [SF01] as the equivalent 4x4 determinant method) and tetrahedral meshes. Usage: Used in ray tracing of triangular meshes and tetrahedral meshes. It is also used in the traversal of tetrahedral meshes for direct volume rendering. Fast Ray Traversal of Tetrahedral and Hexahedral Meshes for Direct Volume Rendering, https://doi.org/10.2312/vissym/eurovis06/235-242, fetched 2026-10-24 Fast Ray–Tetrahedron Intersection using Plücker..., https://users.uop.gr/~nplatis%20/files/PlatisTheoharisRayTetra.pdf, fetched 2026-10-29 (unofficial) RAY TRACING IMPLICIT SURFACES FOR INTERACTIVE..., https://www.sci.utah.edu/~knolla/thesis.pdf, fetched 2026-10-29 (unofficial)
